WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:08.010
NATALIE KENT: Welcome.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:09.380
My name is Natalie Kent.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.770
And I'm a sophomore here
studying English and French.

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:15.680
I'm here this evening to
introduce our new speaker, Dr.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:17.660
Mark Blitz.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:20.090
Mark Blitz is the
Fletcher Jones Professor

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:24.020
of Political Philosophy at
Claremont McKenna College.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:28.430
He earned his AB and PhD
from Harvard University.

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.860
Prior to his appointment
at Claremont McKenna,

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.590
he taught political philosophy
at Harvard University

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:36.180
and the University
of Pennsylvania.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.420
He served in the
Reagan administration

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.940
as Associate Director of the
United States Information

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.250
Agency, where he was
the senior United States

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:45.860
official in charge of
educational and cultural

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:47.900
programs abroad.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.270
Dr. Blitz has also
served as a senior staff

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:53.020
member of the Senate
Committee on Foreign Relations

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:55.690
and as vice president
of the Hudson Institute.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:58.760
He is the author of
numerous books titled

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:02.660
Conserving Liberty, Plato's
Political Philosophy, Duty

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:03.260
Bound--

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.900
Responsibility and
American Public Life,

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.390
and Heidegger's "Being and
Time" and the Possibility

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:10.100
of Political Philosophy.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.416
Ladies and gentlemen, please
welcome Dr. Mark Blitz.

00:01:13.416 --> 00:01:15.896
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:28.792 --> 00:01:32.630
MARK BLITZ: Thanks, Natalie
for that nice introduction.

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:36.070
I once worked in government
for someone very controversial.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:38.860
And whenever he gave
a speech and received

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:41.188
a nice introduction, he
would look around and say,

00:01:41.188 --> 00:01:42.355
I must be in the wrong room.

00:01:42.355 --> 00:01:44.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:47.680
But I'm in the right room.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.520
And thank you all
for being here.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.600
I don't pretend to be
an expert on Confucius,

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.990
although I've
taught his Analects.

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:01.210
But it's not
completely unreasonable

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.850
that I'm talking
to you about him.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:07.990
My area academically is
political philosophy,

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:12.670
specifically Plato and
Aristotle, but also

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:16.830
19th- and 20th-century
German philosophy.

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:19.740
And it turns out that
Aristotle and Plato

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:23.100
are very good jumping-off
points for us in order

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:28.710
to understand and
consider Confucius.

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:31.980
As Natalie mentioned, I
was also, at one point--

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.490
this was during Ronald
Reagan's second term--

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:40.170
in charge of our country's
Academic and Young Leader

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:44.130
exchange programs, the Fulbright
Program, and other programs

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.850
similar to that, and
also of our books

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:52.320
and libraries and
foreign cultural centers,

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.990
kind of the US
equivalent of what

00:02:54.990 --> 00:03:00.540
China now has abroad with
all of its Confucius centers,

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.100
these centers which
they've started

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:06.240
in various universities,
to some degree

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.700
for propagandistic
purposes, to some degree

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.260
to keep tabs on students.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:16.540
But in general, we didn't
do any such things.

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:20.310
The point of our US
programs was what was then

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:24.420
called mutual understanding.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.870
Now they call it soft power.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:32.800
We actually thought of it
during the Reagan administration

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:34.720
as the war of ideas--

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:39.330
the war of ideas,
freedom, capitalism,

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:46.860
liberal democracy on one side,
communism on the other side.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:52.340
So I was, as it were,
an officer in this war

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:54.440
and therefore have
considered something

00:03:54.440 --> 00:04:01.290
about the effect of thought
on a people as a whole,

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:05.860
something relevant obviously
to Confucius and China.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:08.110
Obviously, in a way,
the war of ideas

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:14.920
never ends, as we see
today, even if one prevails

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.779
for a while, as we did.

00:04:17.779 --> 00:04:21.460
But it's a continuing
and ongoing effort.

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:29.480
So Confucius, or more
literally Master Kong--

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:33.740
wonderful name--
lived, they think--

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:38.150
because these things are always
a bit hazy, off a year or two,

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:38.900
lived--

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:43.775
let's say, from 551 to 479 BC.

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:50.070
His family was nobility.

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:54.810
But he was himself not wealthy.

00:04:54.810 --> 00:05:00.000
And he always referred
favorably to the Zhou Dynasty,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.680
which ruled his home state,
which was in Northern China.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:10.310
This rule was breaking
down during his life.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:14.720
He had various minor political
positions, never really

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:19.070
a major one, although it
seems that he and his students

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:21.230
wanted major positions.

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:24.680
That was one of his
goals, not achieved.

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:28.190
And for long periods of time he
was just an itinerant teacher.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:37.570
The facts of his life are
hazy because reports of them

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:39.610
are not really contemporaneous.

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:42.780
They occur much later.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:46.380
Most important for us, the
status of the Analects,

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:49.980
which is the central compilation
of his views, the status

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:54.120
of the Analects, as a
text, as we scholars say,

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.310
is very unclear.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:06:01.860
The present text was compiled
by a much later scholar.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:05.970
Which sayings were
written or said by him

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:08.760
or were compiled from
things he actually

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:11.920
said, who knows for sure.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:14.520
So it would be very hard
to study the Analects

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:20.010
with a kind of precision with
which one would study a novel,

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:24.450
or even one of the works of
Plato, where you can be certain

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:29.310
that the author wrote the thing,
and you can study the work as

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:33.920
if it reflects the
intention of the author.

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:37.130
Unclear when you study
Confucius that you

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:40.130
can do something like that.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:42.740
So scholars try to resolve
these uncertainties

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:47.240
by considering what was
handed down after his death

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:51.320
and then dismissing what seems
historically anachronistic.

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:57.820
The Analects are composed
of 20 books, a bunch of what

00:06:57.820 --> 00:06:59.690
are called chapters
in each books,

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:03.980
but the chapters are just these
short sayings, by and large.

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:10.280
Books 3 through 9 and
11 through 15 of the 20

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:13.100
are generally considered
to be authentic,

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:16.910
and books 4 through 8 are
thought to be the oldest.

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:18.950
But these, I think,
are thoughts,

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:21.770
and no one knows certainly.

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:23.810
So this uncertainty,
as I said, really

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:28.360
affects the precision with
which anyone can study him.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:36.350
Now, Confucius is
significant first

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:40.130
because his thought
or his statements

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:44.540
and those of his disciples
have become so important

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:49.440
in China and in the
countries influenced by it.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:51.950
And second, of course,
he's significant because

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:54.150
of the content of
the thought itself.

00:07:57.270 --> 00:08:02.300
His work went through
various stages of influence.

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:07.820
It became significant,
especially during the dynasty

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:13.210
which began to
rule China in 960.

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:17.230
This was when the civil service
examinations that were based

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:19.195
on Confucius really took hold.

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:27.390
This importance of
Confucius and commentaries

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:32.130
on Confucius for the civil
service examinations, which

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:39.429
helped to rise to the top,
this went on until 1905.

00:08:39.429 --> 00:08:42.549
So Confucius's great
influence comes first

00:08:42.549 --> 00:08:45.610
from the fact that in
order to make your career

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:51.770
and keep your way, you had
to know him for the exams,

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:55.690
and second, because much
of that seeped down.

00:08:55.690 --> 00:09:00.220
Much of what he said then
seeped down to ordinary views.

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:04.660
So even when Mao Zedong
attacked Confucius and Confucian

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:08.230
thinking, which he did,
this didn't really, I think,

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:11.230
take hold because of
the historic influence

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:14.320
and power of the thought.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:17.080
You might think here,
to a much lesser extent,

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:23.140
but nonetheless of the British
Civil Service in its heyday,

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:27.910
let's say, from the last half or
last third of the 19th century

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:32.740
until the Cold War, when
everyone, pretty much, who

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:36.310
went to the schools from
which senior people were drawn

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:39.400
knew the Greek and
Latin classics.

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:42.670
And some knew them very well.

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:45.820
Or you might consider the
upper crust of the French Civil

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:48.970
Service, where they all
study, and they still

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:52.660
study Descartes and the
Enlightenment and so on.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:54.782
And the upper crust of
these civil services

00:09:54.782 --> 00:09:56.740
are extremely important
in running the country.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:04.950
Confucius expresses the point
of this kind of education.

00:10:04.950 --> 00:10:09.570
Rather than a merely
technical education, in one

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:12.050
of his remarks--

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:14.050
let me read it--

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:16.150
"the master said--"
wouldn't it be

00:10:16.150 --> 00:10:18.730
wonderful to have
before anything

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:20.395
you said "the master said?"

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:33.060
"The master said, a gentleman
does not behave as a tool"

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:36.270
The education is not merely an
education to be an implement

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:38.250
or a tool for someone
else, but it's

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.430
to be the kind of education
to lead and govern.

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:54.180
So I mentioned at the
beginning, the likeness

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:56.520
that people sometimes
see to Aristotle

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:59.220
and why Plato and Aristotle
are a good jumping-off point

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:01.530
for understanding Confucius.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:05.280
And I'll begin my discussion of
the substance of the Analects

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:10.360
by saying what, compared
to Aristotle, it is not.

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:13.240
What's the difference?

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:15.810
And I think that'll help
us to place the Analects

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:19.680
intellectually or
philosophically in relation

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:23.590
to something more familiar.

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:26.440
Compared to Aristotle,
there's no development

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:29.160
of practical reason.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:30.870
There's no development
of the elements

00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:33.740
of practical reasoning.

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:37.610
Nor is there a defense
of intellectual reason,

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:40.370
or what Aristotle calls
intellectual virtue.

00:11:40.370 --> 00:11:45.410
As such, there's no defense of
unbridled philosophical inquiry

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:49.550
of the sort one sees in book
10 of Aristotle's Ethics

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:54.730
or in Plato and in The
Republic, in particular.

00:11:54.730 --> 00:12:00.340
Compared to Aristotle, there's
also no systematic discussion

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:04.150
of the elements of justice
or the elements of happiness

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:06.760
or of the virtues
or even friendship.

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:08.230
There's lots of
discussion of some

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:12.800
of those things, but no
systematic discussion.

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:16.790
By that I mean not only
that matters aren't

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:21.390
traced to some ontological
or metaphysical foundation,

00:12:21.390 --> 00:12:22.130
they're not.

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:25.680
But they're not really in
Aristotle either in the Ethics.

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:26.370
I mean that.

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:30.200
But I also mean that the
varieties and alternatives

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:33.860
of virtue, justice, happiness,
are not systematically

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:37.990
traced and categorized,
as Aristotle does.

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:40.040
Compared to Aristotle,
there's also

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:43.310
no discussion really
of the human soul

00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:47.270
and the powers and
abilities of the human soul.

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:51.410
Even Aristotle's elementary
discussion in the Ethics,

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:54.110
let alone Plato's
discussion of the powers

00:12:54.110 --> 00:12:58.565
of the soul in The Republic, or
both of their more fundamental

00:12:58.565 --> 00:13:02.310
discussions in other works.

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:06.260
Compare it to John
Locke, in a way, one

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:09.170
of the great intellectual
founders behind the Declaration

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:10.580
of Independence.

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:12.380
Compared to John
Locke, there are

00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:15.710
in Confucius no
individual rights,

00:13:15.710 --> 00:13:22.250
no individual natural rights, no
tracing of political authority

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:27.350
to natural individual
authority, no opening up

00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:32.420
of property to full,
private acquisition,

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:35.980
and no true consent
of the governed.

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:39.740
There's little valuing
of competition.

00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:49.370
"The master said,
there's nothing

00:13:49.370 --> 00:13:52.880
which gentlemen compete over.

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:56.540
If competition were inevitable,
it would be in archery,

00:13:56.540 --> 00:13:58.690
wouldn't it?

00:13:58.690 --> 00:14:02.810
But they go up bowing and
making way for each other.

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:06.250
And when they come
down, they have a drink.

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:08.930
So even in their
competition with each other,

00:14:08.930 --> 00:14:09.850
they are gentlemen."

00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:15.070
No real competition argued
for is a good thing,

00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:16.505
in a manner similar to ours.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:23.100
In terms of what Confucius
does emphasize in these books

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:25.650
but is not discussed
in Aristotle,

00:14:25.650 --> 00:14:30.570
one obvious difference is
Confucius's emphasis on ritual,

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:37.040
on custom, on forms of dress,
on manners, one might say.

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:41.250
There's nothing at all in
the Ethics of Aristotle

00:14:41.250 --> 00:14:43.680
or in his other
works that's really

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:46.620
like book 10 of the
Analects of Confucius,

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:52.650
which is where a lot of this
discussion of ritual occurs.

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:56.220
So because of all
of these facts,

00:14:56.220 --> 00:15:00.150
the discussions in Confucius
or the sayings in Confucius

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:07.550
often seem more like
exhorting, hortatory statements

00:15:07.550 --> 00:15:09.590
of the sort that you
might occasionally even

00:15:09.590 --> 00:15:12.980
find in elements of
Plato's dialogues.

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:15.050
Before then, Plato
and Socrates dig

00:15:15.050 --> 00:15:18.200
into the real meaning and
intellectual substance

00:15:18.200 --> 00:15:19.985
of those issues.

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:25.390
The Analects are
really about how

00:15:25.390 --> 00:15:32.060
to follow a way, how to follow
a traditional way, in terms

00:15:32.060 --> 00:15:36.040
of what he thinks
is best in that way,

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:41.550
and given whatever choices
are still open to it.

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:45.470
All of that said, lots of
what he recommends that you do

00:15:45.470 --> 00:15:48.020
are, I think, what we
would also recommend.

00:15:53.470 --> 00:15:56.920
To fully explore the
substance of the Analects

00:15:56.920 --> 00:15:59.720
would require a lengthy study.

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:01.940
But I can at least
indicate, I think,

00:16:01.940 --> 00:16:05.000
the major terms,
the major guidelines

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:08.130
of Confucius's thought.

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:10.890
I put it this way in
terms of guidelines,

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:13.920
because the Analytics,
as I just indicated,

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:17.190
are about how to behave,
but in particular, they're

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:20.790
about how to behave
generally if you

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:28.590
have or aspire to a particular
role or a particular station.

00:16:28.590 --> 00:16:32.190
They're not based on
universal individualism,

00:16:32.190 --> 00:16:35.910
where everything is, in
principle, open to everyone.

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:39.720
They're not really based on
the kind of behavior which is,

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:41.490
in principle, open to everyone.

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:45.150
But they're very much
tied to particular roles,

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:48.580
particular status.

00:16:48.580 --> 00:16:50.550
In addition to
considering the Analects

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:53.730
by thinking about
its major guidelines,

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:57.930
one should also see that
Confucius's remarks are often

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:00.210
directed to
particular disciples.

00:17:00.210 --> 00:17:01.860
They're a group of
disciples with whom

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:04.650
he has these discussions
in the Analects,

00:17:04.650 --> 00:17:05.849
and they're different.

00:17:05.849 --> 00:17:09.030
There are particular
qualities, particular limits

00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:11.470
that are mentioned in
the Analects itself.

00:17:11.470 --> 00:17:14.640
So it's not always
clear if a given remark

00:17:14.640 --> 00:17:18.240
is meant to be generally
applicable, as it might seem,

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:21.990
or more particularly applicable
to the one to whom it's

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:24.520
being addressed.

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:28.750
So given it all this,
what are the major terms

00:17:28.750 --> 00:17:32.110
or major thoughts?

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:36.650
There's first the key
human type, the key

00:17:36.650 --> 00:17:42.680
or central human type
that he recommends,

00:17:42.680 --> 00:17:48.440
the gentleman, as opposed
to the petty or small man.

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:49.520
That's the difference.

00:17:52.540 --> 00:18:01.510
To read a couple of
things, "Ji Kang-zi asked

00:18:01.510 --> 00:18:05.560
Master Kong about
government, saying,

00:18:05.560 --> 00:18:10.150
suppose I were to kill those
who lack the way in order

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:12.670
to advance those
who have the way.

00:18:12.670 --> 00:18:14.200
How would that be?

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:17.420
Master Kong replied, you
are running the government.

00:18:17.420 --> 00:18:19.660
So what is the point of killing?

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:23.380
If you desire good, the
people will be good.

00:18:23.380 --> 00:18:27.820
The nature of the
gentlemen is as the wind,

00:18:27.820 --> 00:18:32.410
and the nature of a small
man is as the grass.

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:36.370
When the wind blows over
the grass, it always bends."

00:18:39.790 --> 00:18:43.950
So this is among, of course,
his famous sayings on which you

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:46.340
could reflect for a long time.

00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:52.320
To read something
else about gentlemen,

00:18:52.320 --> 00:19:03.120
which he says, "the
master said, the gentleman

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:07.010
reaches out for what is above.

00:19:07.010 --> 00:19:10.130
The small man reaches
out for what is below."

00:19:14.665 --> 00:19:16.290
You'd rather be a
gentleman, of course.

00:19:20.600 --> 00:19:22.730
There are also
people he calls men

00:19:22.730 --> 00:19:25.250
of quality, those who
have particular virtues,

00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:29.440
particular skills,
those he calls sages,

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:31.690
who have a certain
level of learning even

00:19:31.690 --> 00:19:33.410
beyond the gentleman.

00:19:33.410 --> 00:19:37.210
There's further what he
once calls the complete man.

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:40.120
All of these are high
or desirable types,

00:19:40.120 --> 00:19:44.620
and it's the gentleman
who's most fully described.

00:19:44.620 --> 00:19:49.240
He also discusses the
people who are governed,

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:52.780
various levels of
government officials.

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:55.880
There's very little
on women, by the way.

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:59.320
And the major
statement about women

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:01.650
would be politically
incorrect for us now.

00:20:01.650 --> 00:20:03.860
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:05.320
I therefore won't
read it to you.

00:20:05.320 --> 00:20:07.650
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:18.060
Now, gentlemen are supposed
to embody or exemplify

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:23.430
a characteristic which,
is called ren, R-E-N, when

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:25.870
it's transliterated and so on.

00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:28.410
That's the central quality.

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:33.920
It's often translated as
humaneness or humanity,

00:20:33.920 --> 00:20:38.720
sometimes translated
as generosity.

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:45.480
Benevolence might be better, or
perhaps kind of graciousness,

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:49.240
propriety, refinement.

00:20:49.240 --> 00:20:53.460
You might say it's the
gracious ease or propriety that

00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:57.760
in the past used to define the
gentleman, not just his past,

00:20:57.760 --> 00:21:01.570
but our past as well.

00:21:01.570 --> 00:21:05.770
You might call this
graciousness or humaneness,

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:09.595
the broad substance of virtuous
action in regard to others.

00:21:12.530 --> 00:21:15.950
One reason that graciousness
or benevolence, in a way,

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:20.570
is a useful translation, maybe
more than humaneness simply,

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:23.690
is because this quality
is not something, I think,

00:21:23.690 --> 00:21:28.340
characteristic of all human
beings, as Confucius sees it.

00:21:28.340 --> 00:21:30.680
Again, it's most likely
the graceful ease

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:35.120
and propriety of the
gentleman, in particular.

00:21:35.120 --> 00:21:36.590
To be humane--

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:38.300
I'll use that term--

00:21:38.300 --> 00:21:43.475
is not to be rude or
coarse or devious.

00:21:46.620 --> 00:21:52.370
"The master said, when substance
prevails over refinement,

00:21:52.370 --> 00:21:54.620
there's churlishness.

00:21:54.620 --> 00:21:57.290
And when refinement
or mere refinement

00:21:57.290 --> 00:22:01.370
prevails over substance,
there is pedantry.

00:22:01.370 --> 00:22:05.450
Only if refinement and
substance are properly blended

00:22:05.450 --> 00:22:07.970
does one become a gentleman."

00:22:15.680 --> 00:22:19.310
Now, next point,
really, the virtues

00:22:19.310 --> 00:22:23.810
or humaneness or
benevolence, generally,

00:22:23.810 --> 00:22:30.380
are displayed primarily
in various relationships

00:22:30.380 --> 00:22:37.200
with parents, therefore
familial piety or familial duty,

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:39.590
which is another central
practice, of course,

00:22:39.590 --> 00:22:43.020
in Confucius, and
loyalty generally.

00:22:49.810 --> 00:22:51.610
Master You said--

00:22:51.610 --> 00:22:55.150
Master You is one
of the disciples--

00:22:55.150 --> 00:22:59.140
"few indeed are those
who are naturally filial

00:22:59.140 --> 00:23:02.230
towards their
parents and dutiful

00:23:02.230 --> 00:23:05.950
towards their elder
brothers but are also fond

00:23:05.950 --> 00:23:08.320
of opposing their superiors.

00:23:08.320 --> 00:23:10.910
And it never happens--

00:23:10.910 --> 00:23:13.970
it never happens--
that those who

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:16.520
do not like opposing
their superiors

00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:19.770
are fond of creating
civil disorder."

00:23:19.770 --> 00:23:26.470
It's the kind of so-called
conservatism of Confucius.

00:23:26.470 --> 00:23:29.680
"The gentleman concerns
himself with the root.

00:23:29.680 --> 00:23:33.400
And if the root is firmly
planted, the Way grows.

00:23:33.400 --> 00:23:38.050
Filial piety and fraternal
duty, surely they

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:42.170
are the roots of humaneness."

00:23:42.170 --> 00:23:47.030
"The master said, young men
should be filial when at home

00:23:47.030 --> 00:23:50.480
and respectful to elders
when away from home.

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:53.480
They should be earnest
and trustworthy.

00:23:53.480 --> 00:23:56.120
Although they should love
the multitude far and wide,

00:23:56.120 --> 00:23:58.805
they should be intimate
only with the humane."

00:24:05.190 --> 00:24:06.210
I'll read one other.

00:24:12.310 --> 00:24:19.190
"The Duke of Qi told Master
Kong, in my locality,

00:24:19.190 --> 00:24:21.740
there's a certain paragon.

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:25.370
For when his father stole
a sheep, he, the son,

00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:28.070
bore witness against him.

00:24:28.070 --> 00:24:33.170
Master Kong said, in my
locality, those who are upright

00:24:33.170 --> 00:24:35.240
are different from this.

00:24:35.240 --> 00:24:40.490
Fathers cover up for their
sons, and sons cover up

00:24:40.490 --> 00:24:42.290
for their fathers.

00:24:42.290 --> 00:24:46.136
Uprightness is to
be found in this."

00:24:46.136 --> 00:24:50.150
Yeah, and that can be compared
with a well-known dialogue

00:24:50.150 --> 00:24:55.370
on piety of Plato's called
the Euthyphro, whose action

00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:58.040
concerns this kind of thing.

00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:03.030
It's not clear, I
think, from all this

00:25:03.030 --> 00:25:06.060
that piety towards
one's father is

00:25:06.060 --> 00:25:10.050
meant to be as complete
morally or as oppressive

00:25:10.050 --> 00:25:13.735
as it seems to have become in
some later periods in China.

00:25:16.410 --> 00:25:23.580
In loyalty, one also displays
other characteristics,

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:27.900
friendliness, honesty,
or trustworthiness.

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:34.200
Again, gentlemanly
or virtuous actions

00:25:34.200 --> 00:25:38.460
are displayed and are connected
to these people and functions.

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:40.800
They're not merely,
for Confucius,

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:43.200
a general set of
qualities that follow

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:45.630
some sort of universal law.

00:25:45.630 --> 00:25:50.590
As I said, Confucius
does not trace

00:25:50.590 --> 00:25:54.590
the virtues and the qualities
to their intellectual roots.

00:25:54.590 --> 00:26:00.340
They are recommended and, to
a degree, they're described.

00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:03.070
But they're not really
defended philosophically.

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:09.360
Now, as I've said and
have just been saying,

00:26:09.360 --> 00:26:12.750
the favored human types,
let's say the gentlemen,

00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:15.930
the ones to which one aspires,
even if you can't quite

00:26:15.930 --> 00:26:19.860
reach it, the
gentleman and the sage,

00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:24.780
all of these practice virtuous
actions not abstractly

00:26:24.780 --> 00:26:27.300
or as objects of
universal rules,

00:26:27.300 --> 00:26:32.610
but rather they practice
them always within a context.

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:35.620
And the context-- and this
is really the next point.

00:26:35.620 --> 00:26:38.250
The context is
primarily or directly

00:26:38.250 --> 00:26:43.810
within ritual behavior
or customary ritual.

00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:47.100
Li-- L-I-- is the term
that's usually translated

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:49.260
in one of those ways.

00:26:49.260 --> 00:26:52.740
That's also connected to
reverence and courteous

00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:54.660
behavior.

00:26:54.660 --> 00:26:59.610
But it's not-- at least it
seems not to be in Confucius--

00:26:59.610 --> 00:27:03.330
religious worship of
gods or direct concern

00:27:03.330 --> 00:27:04.530
with an afterlife.

00:27:04.530 --> 00:27:10.530
Where Confucius is on that
issue is a deep question

00:27:10.530 --> 00:27:11.730
for those who study him.

00:27:14.460 --> 00:27:18.210
Ritual behavior also
reflects and involves

00:27:18.210 --> 00:27:20.130
refined and cultured behavior.

00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:25.510
So to sum this up,
the central element

00:27:25.510 --> 00:27:29.530
of Confucius's teaching,
what is central

00:27:29.530 --> 00:27:34.930
is graciousness or propriety
in customary practices

00:27:34.930 --> 00:27:36.970
and customary relations.

00:27:36.970 --> 00:27:42.340
That really is kind of the
central substantive element

00:27:42.340 --> 00:27:46.210
of Confucius's set of teachings.

00:27:46.210 --> 00:27:49.870
To read again some
of that, Zhonggong

00:27:49.870 --> 00:27:51.490
asked about humaneness.

00:27:51.490 --> 00:27:56.110
"The master said, when you are
away from home, behave as if

00:27:56.110 --> 00:27:58.450
receiving an important guest.

00:27:58.450 --> 00:28:00.940
Employ the people as
if you were officiating

00:28:00.940 --> 00:28:02.830
at a great sacrifice.

00:28:02.830 --> 00:28:08.270
Do not impose on others what
you would not like yourself.

00:28:08.270 --> 00:28:11.510
Then there will be no
resentment against you,

00:28:11.510 --> 00:28:14.210
either in the state
or in the family.

00:28:14.210 --> 00:28:17.420
Zhonggong said,
although I'm not clever,

00:28:17.420 --> 00:28:20.120
I beg to put this
advice into practice."

00:28:26.050 --> 00:28:30.580
Fan Chi, another disciple,
asked about humaneness.

00:28:30.580 --> 00:28:35.980
"The master said, courtesy
in private life, reverence

00:28:35.980 --> 00:28:41.150
in handling business, loyalty
and relations with others,

00:28:41.150 --> 00:28:44.200
this should not be
set aside, even if one

00:28:44.200 --> 00:28:46.875
visits the barbarian tribes."

00:28:46.875 --> 00:28:47.375
Mm-hmm.

00:28:50.050 --> 00:28:57.930
And a final one in this
group, Master Kong said,

00:28:57.930 --> 00:29:02.640
"there are nine things the
gentleman concentrates on.

00:29:02.640 --> 00:29:05.400
In seeing, he
concentrates on clarity.

00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:08.460
In listening, he
concentrates on acuteness.

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:11.580
In expression, he
concentrates on warmness.

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:14.760
In demeanor, he
concentrates on courtesy.

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:17.580
In words, he
concentrates on loyalty.

00:29:17.580 --> 00:29:21.270
In deeds, he concentrates
on reverence.

00:29:21.270 --> 00:29:23.130
When he's in doubt,
he concentrates

00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:25.200
on asking questions.

00:29:25.200 --> 00:29:28.160
When he's indignant, he
concentrates on the problems--"

00:29:28.160 --> 00:29:30.540
kind of the obstacles
which would slow you down,

00:29:30.540 --> 00:29:33.240
slow down your indignity.

00:29:33.240 --> 00:29:36.270
"And when he sees
opportunity for gain,

00:29:36.270 --> 00:29:39.840
he concentrates
on what is right,"

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:44.040
a hard group of things, I think,
to actually do so completely.

00:29:47.940 --> 00:29:50.820
Now, the next
point, all of this,

00:29:50.820 --> 00:29:53.070
again let's call
it gentlemanliness

00:29:53.070 --> 00:29:55.890
and humaneness, all
of this comes together

00:29:55.890 --> 00:30:01.050
only with learning moral
and cultural, as well as,

00:30:01.050 --> 00:30:05.235
to a degree, military and
mathematical learning.

00:30:09.290 --> 00:30:14.810
"The master said, you have heard
the six sayings about the six

00:30:14.810 --> 00:30:17.170
hidden consequences.

00:30:17.170 --> 00:30:21.070
When he replied that he had not,
the master went on, sit down,

00:30:21.070 --> 00:30:23.080
and I will tell you.

00:30:23.080 --> 00:30:27.070
If one loves humaneness
but does not love learning,

00:30:27.070 --> 00:30:29.850
the consequence
of this is folly.

00:30:29.850 --> 00:30:33.420
If one loves understanding
but does not love learning,

00:30:33.420 --> 00:30:37.020
the consequence of
this is unorthodoxy.

00:30:37.020 --> 00:30:41.160
If one loves good faith
but does not love learning,

00:30:41.160 --> 00:30:44.730
the consequence of this
is damaging behavior.

00:30:44.730 --> 00:30:48.900
If one loves straightforwardness
but does not love learning,

00:30:48.900 --> 00:30:52.050
the consequence of
this is rudeness.

00:30:52.050 --> 00:30:55.830
If one loves courage but
does not love learning,

00:30:55.830 --> 00:30:58.980
the consequence of
this is rebelliousness.

00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:02.250
If one loves strength but
does not love learning,

00:31:02.250 --> 00:31:04.470
the consequence of
this is violence."

00:31:07.690 --> 00:31:10.870
So people can, of course
deviate from propriety.

00:31:10.870 --> 00:31:13.840
They probably
usually do, in fact.

00:31:13.840 --> 00:31:16.900
And Confucius's
interest, his concern

00:31:16.900 --> 00:31:19.765
is to restore us to what he
thinks is the proper way.

00:31:22.550 --> 00:31:25.130
This proper way,
he also indicates,

00:31:25.130 --> 00:31:31.790
actually occurred earlier
in the reign of a Duke Zhou.

00:31:31.790 --> 00:31:36.590
The perfect or best time,
as Confucius sees it,

00:31:36.590 --> 00:31:39.140
is actually thought
of as something

00:31:39.140 --> 00:31:42.590
which once concretely existed.

00:31:42.590 --> 00:31:46.420
He doesn't project the kind
of impossible best government.

00:31:46.420 --> 00:31:48.250
Rather, the best
government is something,

00:31:48.250 --> 00:31:50.920
he claims, actually to
have existed in the past.

00:31:53.720 --> 00:31:58.000
In fact, Confucius
thinks of himself

00:31:58.000 --> 00:32:02.700
as a transmitter
not as a creator.

00:32:02.700 --> 00:32:06.840
"The master said, I
transmit but do not create.

00:32:06.840 --> 00:32:11.610
Being fond of the truth, I
am at admirer of antiquity."

00:32:16.910 --> 00:32:22.240
"The master said, if by
keeping the old warm,

00:32:22.240 --> 00:32:25.150
one can provide
understanding of the new.

00:32:25.150 --> 00:32:26.410
One is fit to be a teacher."

00:32:30.660 --> 00:32:34.860
That's a nice thought,
as so many of these are.

00:32:39.110 --> 00:32:42.920
So one can see in all of
this that he exhorts us

00:32:42.920 --> 00:32:46.130
to a way of life that
consists of proper behavior

00:32:46.130 --> 00:32:49.250
in different activities
or functions or actions,

00:32:49.250 --> 00:32:52.880
proper ways that emphasize
ritual and family

00:32:52.880 --> 00:32:56.980
or filial piety, not
radical individualism.

00:32:56.980 --> 00:33:00.440
And that points to virtuous
action, a little bit less

00:33:00.440 --> 00:33:04.040
as character simply, and
more in terms of, again

00:33:04.040 --> 00:33:07.520
gentlemanly propriety
and refinement,

00:33:07.520 --> 00:33:13.840
even a kind of mannered
formalism in activity.

00:33:13.840 --> 00:33:16.300
That's important for the
following reason for him.

00:33:16.300 --> 00:33:19.690
You might wish to act
correctly, but what actually

00:33:19.690 --> 00:33:23.230
is correct behavior
also depends on how

00:33:23.230 --> 00:33:28.040
others understand what you're
doing and how you see them.

00:33:28.040 --> 00:33:32.620
And that's one reason why
observing custom and ritual

00:33:32.620 --> 00:33:38.510
can be important for
displaying proper respect.

00:33:38.510 --> 00:33:43.490
This is from book 8,
chapter 2, "the master said,

00:33:43.490 --> 00:33:46.960
if one is courteous but
does without ritual,

00:33:46.960 --> 00:33:49.840
then one dissipates
one's energy.

00:33:49.840 --> 00:33:53.590
If one is cautious but
does without ritual,

00:33:53.590 --> 00:33:55.030
then one becomes timid.

00:33:55.030 --> 00:33:58.100
If one is bold but
does without ritual,

00:33:58.100 --> 00:33:59.770
then one becomes reckless.

00:33:59.770 --> 00:34:02.860
If one is forthright
but does without ritual,

00:34:02.860 --> 00:34:05.470
then one becomes rude."

00:34:05.470 --> 00:34:07.840
Hence the need for
ritual to actually bring

00:34:07.840 --> 00:34:11.739
about proper action,
whatever one's intention.

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:20.370
Because government,
politics, government,

00:34:20.370 --> 00:34:25.409
is such an interest of ours,
let me discuss Confucius's views

00:34:25.409 --> 00:34:27.780
on this further.

00:34:27.780 --> 00:34:30.389
Confucius's sayings
on this are largely

00:34:30.389 --> 00:34:34.389
about officials in government
and their behavior,

00:34:34.389 --> 00:34:37.590
how they should act,
what their relations are.

00:34:37.590 --> 00:34:41.760
Central is trust
and the similarity

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:47.060
between action in the family
and activity in government.

00:34:52.090 --> 00:34:56.170
His remarks are not really
very much about institutions,

00:34:56.170 --> 00:35:00.570
or even about justice
directly, but primarily

00:35:00.570 --> 00:35:03.600
about behavior and action.

00:35:03.600 --> 00:35:05.370
Let me read a few
things to give you

00:35:05.370 --> 00:35:08.370
a sense of what his
views on this are.

00:35:14.380 --> 00:35:19.300
"Duke Ding asked how rulers
should employ ministers

00:35:19.300 --> 00:35:21.760
and how ministers
should serve rulers.

00:35:21.760 --> 00:35:25.750
Master Kong replied, rulers
and employing ministers

00:35:25.750 --> 00:35:29.070
do so in accordance with ritual.

00:35:29.070 --> 00:35:33.627
And ministers, in serving
rulers, do so in accordance

00:35:33.627 --> 00:35:34.210
with loyalty."

00:35:39.230 --> 00:35:42.120
[? Zi Zhong ?] asked
about government.

00:35:42.120 --> 00:35:46.140
"The master said, if
there's enough food,

00:35:46.140 --> 00:35:49.290
and if there are enough
weapons, the people

00:35:49.290 --> 00:35:51.960
will put their trust in it.

00:35:51.960 --> 00:35:54.930
[? Zi Zhong ?] said,
suppose you definitely

00:35:54.930 --> 00:35:59.850
had no alternative but to give
up one of these three, food,

00:35:59.850 --> 00:36:03.365
weapons, trust, which
would you relinquish first?

00:36:06.310 --> 00:36:09.550
The master said, I
would give up weapons.

00:36:09.550 --> 00:36:13.690
[? Zi Zhong ?] said, suppose you
definitely had no alternative

00:36:13.690 --> 00:36:16.000
but to give up one
of the remaining two,

00:36:16.000 --> 00:36:18.160
which would you
relinquish first?

00:36:18.160 --> 00:36:21.990
The master said, I
would give up food.

00:36:21.990 --> 00:36:25.560
From of old, death
comes to all men.

00:36:25.560 --> 00:36:29.865
But a people will not
stand if it lacks trust."

00:36:33.830 --> 00:36:42.770
And then I'll read a
couple more on government.

00:36:42.770 --> 00:36:45.530
"Zhonggong being
steward to the Ji family

00:36:45.530 --> 00:36:47.090
asked about government.

00:36:47.090 --> 00:36:50.210
The master said, give a
lead to your officials,

00:36:50.210 --> 00:36:54.560
pardon minor errors, and promote
men of quality and talent.

00:36:54.560 --> 00:36:58.670
He said, how does one recognize
men of quality and talent

00:36:58.670 --> 00:37:00.560
so as to promote them?"

00:37:00.560 --> 00:37:05.540
He said, "the master said,
promote those you do recognize,

00:37:05.540 --> 00:37:09.170
for will others neglect
those you do not recognize?"

00:37:12.460 --> 00:37:14.470
You can decide how
helpful that answer is.

00:37:14.470 --> 00:37:16.795
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:19.590 --> 00:37:20.215
And one more.

00:37:27.170 --> 00:37:30.110
"Jikangzi asked,
how the people might

00:37:30.110 --> 00:37:33.210
be induced to be
respectful and loyal,

00:37:33.210 --> 00:37:35.000
so they might be
properly encouraged?

00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:38.330
The master said, if you
oversee them with dignity,

00:37:38.330 --> 00:37:39.980
they will be respectful.

00:37:39.980 --> 00:37:42.350
If you're dutiful towards
your parents and kind

00:37:42.350 --> 00:37:45.200
to your children, then
they will be loyal.

00:37:45.200 --> 00:37:48.990
If you promote the good and
instruct the incompetent,

00:37:48.990 --> 00:37:51.450
then they will be encouraged."

00:37:58.460 --> 00:38:00.800
Let me now say
something very briefly

00:38:00.800 --> 00:38:04.640
about the following question.

00:38:04.640 --> 00:38:06.650
What, given all
this, could one say

00:38:06.650 --> 00:38:10.940
about Confucius and
contemporary Chinese politics

00:38:10.940 --> 00:38:12.770
and government?

00:38:12.770 --> 00:38:15.140
During Mao's reign,
as I said, Confucius

00:38:15.140 --> 00:38:18.340
was condemned as a reactionary.

00:38:18.340 --> 00:38:20.480
But now the rulers
celebrate him.

00:38:20.480 --> 00:38:23.320
As I said, they call
their institutes Confucian

00:38:23.320 --> 00:38:24.160
institutes.

00:38:26.810 --> 00:38:29.870
The question is, whether
or not Confucius's writings

00:38:29.870 --> 00:38:34.610
are a force for individual
freedom, rights, privacy,

00:38:34.610 --> 00:38:37.750
and independence.

00:38:37.750 --> 00:38:41.580
He's certainly a force for
virtue, humaneness, learning,

00:38:41.580 --> 00:38:43.590
trust, and friendship.

00:38:43.590 --> 00:38:46.410
And in that sense, just
as Aristotle would be,

00:38:46.410 --> 00:38:50.220
against tyranny,
Confucius favors

00:38:50.220 --> 00:38:53.190
a kind of gentlemanliness.

00:38:53.190 --> 00:38:55.590
Confucius also
distinguishes governments

00:38:55.590 --> 00:38:59.130
that follow the right
way from those that

00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:00.690
do not follow the right way.

00:39:03.750 --> 00:39:07.860
Confucius also argues in
favor of the importance

00:39:07.860 --> 00:39:11.010
of the proper alignment
of names and things,

00:39:11.010 --> 00:39:14.280
the so-called
rectification of names,

00:39:14.280 --> 00:39:18.990
of calling political and social
things what they actually are.

00:39:18.990 --> 00:39:21.780
One might say he
opposes, in advance, some

00:39:21.780 --> 00:39:23.930
of our political correctness.

00:39:27.790 --> 00:39:31.600
Moreover, although humaneness
is practiced socially,

00:39:31.600 --> 00:39:37.140
it's also ultimately in one's
own power to try to seek it.

00:39:37.140 --> 00:39:40.680
But as I said, we do
not see in Confucius

00:39:40.680 --> 00:39:46.100
the openness of individual
desire and satisfaction.

00:39:46.100 --> 00:39:48.950
And there's a limit, which comes
from the great importance he

00:39:48.950 --> 00:39:52.970
places on the kind of given
political and social roles.

00:39:55.830 --> 00:40:00.020
So I would say, but
anyone could disagree,

00:40:00.020 --> 00:40:04.010
that Confucius, the actual
Confucius of the Analects, is,

00:40:04.010 --> 00:40:07.310
on the whole, healthy
or salutary force

00:40:07.310 --> 00:40:11.520
for good politics,
but not simply.

00:40:11.520 --> 00:40:13.800
Healthy because you
could not honestly

00:40:13.800 --> 00:40:18.450
say that the current
leaders are expressing

00:40:18.450 --> 00:40:22.650
proper Confucian qualities.

00:40:22.650 --> 00:40:26.110
So I'll say a
couple more things,

00:40:26.110 --> 00:40:30.070
and then we can
have some questions.

00:40:33.530 --> 00:40:38.930
Confucius took many
characteristics seriously,

00:40:38.930 --> 00:40:41.600
and he wanted them for himself.

00:40:41.600 --> 00:40:45.680
And in many ways these
self understandings

00:40:45.680 --> 00:40:48.890
are seen as exemplary
for other people as well.

00:40:52.780 --> 00:40:56.670
"The master said, the failure
to cultivate virtue, the failure

00:40:56.670 --> 00:40:59.430
to put it into
practice, the failure

00:40:59.430 --> 00:41:03.180
to put into practice what I've
learned, hearing what is right

00:41:03.180 --> 00:41:05.940
and being unable
to move towards it,

00:41:05.940 --> 00:41:08.850
being unable to change
what's not good,

00:41:08.850 --> 00:41:10.005
these are my worries."

00:41:16.510 --> 00:41:19.840
"The master said,
in making an effort,

00:41:19.840 --> 00:41:22.250
I am comparable with others.

00:41:22.250 --> 00:41:25.270
But as to myself being
a gentleman in practice,

00:41:25.270 --> 00:41:28.540
I have never yet managed
to achieve that."

00:41:28.540 --> 00:41:30.930
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:35.240 --> 00:41:39.560
"The master said, at 15, I
set my heart on learning.

00:41:39.560 --> 00:41:41.210
At 30, I was established.

00:41:41.210 --> 00:41:41.790
At 40.

00:41:41.790 --> 00:41:43.490
I had no perplexities.

00:41:43.490 --> 00:41:46.100
At 50, I understood
the decrees of heaven.

00:41:46.100 --> 00:41:48.560
At 60, my ear was in accord.

00:41:48.560 --> 00:41:52.280
And at 70, I followed what
my heart desired but did

00:41:52.280 --> 00:41:55.295
not transgress what was right."

00:42:00.240 --> 00:42:02.430
"The master said,
do I, for my part,

00:42:02.430 --> 00:42:04.710
really possess understanding?

00:42:04.710 --> 00:42:07.770
No, I do not possess
understanding.

00:42:07.770 --> 00:42:10.470
But if there's an ordinary
person putting a question

00:42:10.470 --> 00:42:14.610
to me, although his mind
seems to be quite blank,

00:42:14.610 --> 00:42:17.610
I hammer at both
sides of the question

00:42:17.610 --> 00:42:20.860
and go into it thoroughly."

00:42:20.860 --> 00:42:25.690
Confucius also took music
and poetry very seriously

00:42:25.690 --> 00:42:29.530
and the beauty connected to them
and the proper behavior that's

00:42:29.530 --> 00:42:31.450
sometimes connected to them.

00:42:31.450 --> 00:42:34.000
In this, he's like Plato.

00:42:34.000 --> 00:42:37.090
But he doesn't think through, or
attempt even to think through,

00:42:37.090 --> 00:42:42.760
universal characteristics by
which one could specify beauty.

00:42:42.760 --> 00:42:47.560
I'll conclude with his
humor, or even his sarcasm,

00:42:47.560 --> 00:42:50.920
and a couple of my
favorite sayings,

00:42:50.920 --> 00:42:54.840
in addition to the
ones I've read.

00:42:54.840 --> 00:43:00.440
"Ji Wen-zi thought three
times before acting.

00:43:00.440 --> 00:43:04.825
When the master heard of this
he said, twice will be enough."

00:43:04.825 --> 00:43:07.250
[LAUGHTER]

00:43:10.170 --> 00:43:11.135
And a couple of others.

00:43:17.010 --> 00:43:24.250
"The master said, if a man
does not anticipate deception

00:43:24.250 --> 00:43:29.210
and does not reckon on bad
faith, but on the other hand,

00:43:29.210 --> 00:43:34.900
is aware in good time when they
occur, he is a man of quality,

00:43:34.900 --> 00:43:35.500
isn't he?"

00:43:39.030 --> 00:43:47.480
And then finally, 2, 17,
"the master said, You--"

00:43:47.480 --> 00:43:51.470
this is one of the disciples,
whose nickname is You--

00:43:51.470 --> 00:43:55.580
"You, shall I teach you about
understanding something?

00:43:55.580 --> 00:43:58.820
When you understand
something, to recognize

00:43:58.820 --> 00:44:00.780
that you understand it.

00:44:00.780 --> 00:44:03.380
But when you do not
understand something,

00:44:03.380 --> 00:44:06.920
to recognize that you
do not understand it.

00:44:06.920 --> 00:44:08.810
That is understanding."

00:44:08.810 --> 00:44:11.100
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:14.780 --> 00:44:18.450
And then actually this
is the final one now

00:44:18.450 --> 00:44:25.260
because I can't resist this,
especially in this context.

00:44:25.260 --> 00:44:28.740
"The master said, it
is not easy to find

00:44:28.740 --> 00:44:31.470
anyone who studies
for three years

00:44:31.470 --> 00:44:33.410
but is not intent on a salary."

00:44:33.410 --> 00:44:35.378
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:37.350 --> 00:44:39.660
So that's Confucius
in a nutshell.

00:44:39.660 --> 00:44:40.160
Thank you.

00:44:40.160 --> 00:44:43.004
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:48.692 --> 00:44:50.980
NATALIE KENT: Thank
you, Dr. Blitz.

00:44:50.980 --> 00:44:53.605
We now have time
for a few questions.

00:44:53.605 --> 00:44:55.230
Please make your way
to the microphone,

00:44:55.230 --> 00:44:56.188
if you have a question.

00:45:09.014 --> 00:45:11.090
AUDIENCE: Aristotle
was the one that

00:45:11.090 --> 00:45:15.840
discovered man's mind and
reason and understanding

00:45:15.840 --> 00:45:18.430
the world that way.

00:45:18.430 --> 00:45:20.640
Aren't you giving
Confucius too much credit

00:45:20.640 --> 00:45:22.890
by comparing him to Aristotle?

00:45:22.890 --> 00:45:28.940
Because Aristotle is unique in
the known history of the world.

00:45:28.940 --> 00:45:30.990
MARK BLITZ: I try
to lay out some

00:45:30.990 --> 00:45:34.800
of the differences between
Aristotle and Confucius.

00:45:34.800 --> 00:45:39.450
The grounds of comparison come
from the relative similarity

00:45:39.450 --> 00:45:40.000
in time--

00:45:40.000 --> 00:45:43.080
Confucius is older-- and
the listing of the virtues

00:45:43.080 --> 00:45:45.840
and the listing of the human
qualities and the thinking

00:45:45.840 --> 00:45:47.550
through of those qualities.

00:45:47.550 --> 00:45:51.130
That's really the substance
of the comparison.

00:45:51.130 --> 00:45:55.500
But in a way, it's precisely
the philosophical underpinnings

00:45:55.500 --> 00:45:58.110
that you don't see in
Confucius and the full working

00:45:58.110 --> 00:46:01.980
out of the various
dimensions and possibilities

00:46:01.980 --> 00:46:04.690
of the virtues,
happiness, and so on.

00:46:04.690 --> 00:46:10.050
So I think I'm giving
Aristotle his proper rank.

00:46:15.100 --> 00:46:17.400
AUDIENCE: You mentioned the--

00:46:17.400 --> 00:46:18.000
over here.

00:46:18.000 --> 00:46:18.890
Over here.

00:46:18.890 --> 00:46:21.554
MARK BLITZ: I see.

00:46:21.554 --> 00:46:24.140
AUDIENCE: Exit stage
right, or something.

00:46:24.140 --> 00:46:27.500
You had mentioned that
during Mao's time,

00:46:27.500 --> 00:46:31.220
he tried to eliminate
references to Confucius.

00:46:31.220 --> 00:46:33.770
Of course there was the
famous Little Red Book of Mao

00:46:33.770 --> 00:46:37.010
during the Cultural Revolution.

00:46:37.010 --> 00:46:42.320
In your knowledge
of Confucius, do you

00:46:42.320 --> 00:46:45.500
know whether any of
his particular sayings

00:46:45.500 --> 00:46:47.120
were plagiarized by Mao?

00:46:50.000 --> 00:46:52.010
MARK BLITZ: No, I
don't know concretely.

00:46:52.010 --> 00:46:55.640
But I wouldn't be surprised.

00:46:55.640 --> 00:47:02.870
Mao also, in the
condemnation of Confucius,

00:47:02.870 --> 00:47:04.970
had everyone read
Confucius to see

00:47:04.970 --> 00:47:10.990
all the things that were wrong,
which was maybe not so wise.

00:47:10.990 --> 00:47:16.582
And the new group is wiser about
those matters, I would say.

00:47:16.582 --> 00:47:19.040
But I would really have to look
carefully at The Little Red

00:47:19.040 --> 00:47:22.580
Book and other works of
Mao to answer that question

00:47:22.580 --> 00:47:25.004
concretely.

00:47:25.004 --> 00:47:27.233
AUDIENCE: So speaking
of the new regime

00:47:27.233 --> 00:47:28.900
and how they've more
or less capitulated

00:47:28.900 --> 00:47:32.320
to the cultural history
of Confucianism,

00:47:32.320 --> 00:47:35.710
give me insights on how they've
managed to sort of round

00:47:35.710 --> 00:47:37.210
the square peg
there around stuff

00:47:37.210 --> 00:47:39.340
like, for example,
calling it like it is.

00:47:39.340 --> 00:47:42.610
Stuff like that's not very
common with Soviet-style

00:47:42.610 --> 00:47:46.330
totalitarian communist regimes.

00:47:46.330 --> 00:47:47.950
MARK BLITZ: There
is this element

00:47:47.950 --> 00:47:53.180
in Confucius, where you act
as you should in relation

00:47:53.180 --> 00:47:56.150
to the role that you have.

00:47:56.150 --> 00:47:59.060
Confucius is not revolutionary.

00:47:59.060 --> 00:48:03.170
He's not radical, really, in
his thinking, and even the way

00:48:03.170 --> 00:48:06.020
that, let's say, Plato is.

00:48:06.020 --> 00:48:12.500
So I think this you can work
with elements in Confucius

00:48:12.500 --> 00:48:14.240
in a useful way.

00:48:14.240 --> 00:48:17.540
But in the long run, if people
take Confucius seriously,

00:48:17.540 --> 00:48:19.700
they will see the difference
between the government

00:48:19.700 --> 00:48:22.160
they have and the
virtues that are

00:48:22.160 --> 00:48:24.860
being suggested by Confucius.

00:48:24.860 --> 00:48:27.410
So there's a lot of
material that you

00:48:27.410 --> 00:48:32.510
can work with in sort of
incorporating Confucius

00:48:32.510 --> 00:48:35.320
into your argument
and your propaganda.

00:48:39.340 --> 00:48:40.340
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:48:40.340 --> 00:48:44.000
Thank you for your speech today.

00:48:44.000 --> 00:48:47.000
Would you say that
the current regime

00:48:47.000 --> 00:48:49.790
has tried to capture
Confucianism,

00:48:49.790 --> 00:48:56.370
the way that the left here in
America is captured as liberal?

00:48:56.370 --> 00:48:59.300
I, even here, can
see conservatives

00:48:59.300 --> 00:49:04.460
speak about the left as if they
deserved the title liberal.

00:49:04.460 --> 00:49:06.710
And I'm wondering if you
could make that comparison?

00:49:11.130 --> 00:49:13.150
MARK BLITZ: For a
while, the general kind

00:49:13.150 --> 00:49:17.880
of category for our regime is
indeed a liberal democracy.

00:49:17.880 --> 00:49:19.660
So it's not accurate,
right, as you're

00:49:19.660 --> 00:49:22.630
suggesting, for Liberalism,
in the true sense,

00:49:22.630 --> 00:49:25.850
to be captured by the left.

00:49:25.850 --> 00:49:28.960
And even the left of this
kind of left-right divide,

00:49:28.960 --> 00:49:35.800
which kind of became formal, in
a way, in the 1820s or 1830s,

00:49:35.800 --> 00:49:40.490
the liberal element there was a
pro-business and pro-parliament

00:49:40.490 --> 00:49:40.990
element.

00:49:40.990 --> 00:49:45.190
So, yeah, there is something
wrong about the way in which we

00:49:45.190 --> 00:49:48.910
talk about our
politics, for sure.

00:49:48.910 --> 00:49:52.610
Yes, I think the goal
of the current regime--

00:49:52.610 --> 00:49:55.180
and I think that's true of, in
a way, any thoughtful regime,

00:49:55.180 --> 00:50:00.550
is to try to have behind you
as much as you can that you

00:50:00.550 --> 00:50:02.920
think the people won't give up.

00:50:02.920 --> 00:50:07.330
So radical as you
might be, it may often

00:50:07.330 --> 00:50:10.570
be the case that you
want to show elements

00:50:10.570 --> 00:50:14.260
of what you're doing as
continuous with what's

00:50:14.260 --> 00:50:15.970
been there in the past.

00:50:15.970 --> 00:50:18.850
How successful they'll
be, I don't know.

00:50:18.850 --> 00:50:25.250
That would be a prediction
that would be hard to make.

00:50:25.250 --> 00:50:25.870
So we'll see.

00:50:28.462 --> 00:50:30.780
AUDIENCE: You mentioned
weapons, so I know

00:50:30.780 --> 00:50:31.920
Confucius thought about it.

00:50:31.920 --> 00:50:34.830
Could you share some
sayings on his ideas

00:50:34.830 --> 00:50:36.325
about war and conflict?

00:50:40.161 --> 00:50:43.110
MARK BLITZ: So archery
is one of the things

00:50:43.110 --> 00:50:45.090
that you're supposed to study.

00:50:45.090 --> 00:50:48.960
Confucius was not ultimately
that interested in war.

00:50:48.960 --> 00:50:52.080
He's not really, I think,
where you'd look for strategy,

00:50:52.080 --> 00:50:55.290
as there are, of course, other
great works that you look at.

00:50:55.290 --> 00:51:00.660
It's secondary always to
virtue, you might say.

00:51:00.660 --> 00:51:03.030
Confucius is not
against punishment.

00:51:03.030 --> 00:51:05.430
But punishment is
secondary to virtue also.

00:51:05.430 --> 00:51:06.940
It shouldn't be necessary.

00:51:06.940 --> 00:51:10.500
So Confucius is more in that
direction and not so much

00:51:10.500 --> 00:51:13.320
in the direction of
war and conflict.

00:51:13.320 --> 00:51:17.250
Harmony would come
closer to what he might

00:51:17.250 --> 00:51:19.671
desire than conflict would.

00:51:19.671 --> 00:51:23.439
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
pacifist [INAUDIBLE]??

00:51:23.439 --> 00:51:25.540
MARK BLITZ: No, he's
certainly not a pacifist.

00:51:25.540 --> 00:51:28.020
And he, himself,
engaged in certain kind

00:51:28.020 --> 00:51:29.160
of political activities.

00:51:29.160 --> 00:51:30.720
He's not a pacifist.

00:51:30.720 --> 00:51:34.260
But war is not his
favorite option.

00:51:34.260 --> 00:51:36.360
There are a lot of
wars then, though.

00:51:36.360 --> 00:51:40.110
You needed to fight in
order to preserve yourself

00:51:40.110 --> 00:51:41.640
and to preserve your dynasty.

00:51:41.640 --> 00:51:50.220
So pacifism, no, but war as
something desirable, not much.

00:51:50.220 --> 00:51:52.440
Courage is one of the
virtues, but it's not

00:51:52.440 --> 00:51:55.500
one that goes all that far by
itself either, but certainly

00:51:55.500 --> 00:51:56.085
not pacifism.

00:52:00.756 --> 00:52:03.090
AUDIENCE: Probably like
many of us here in the room,

00:52:03.090 --> 00:52:05.790
I graduated from college in '69.

00:52:05.790 --> 00:52:10.660
And Chairman Mao's Little Red
Book was kind of everywhere,

00:52:10.660 --> 00:52:14.110
even though I was on a
relatively safe campus,

00:52:14.110 --> 00:52:15.830
so to speak.

00:52:15.830 --> 00:52:17.880
And I wasn't reminded
of that until one

00:52:17.880 --> 00:52:20.940
of Obama's initial
appointees declared

00:52:20.940 --> 00:52:26.970
that Mao and Mother Teresa
were her favorite philosophers.

00:52:26.970 --> 00:52:32.730
And is there anyone outside of
people in Berkeley and Harvard

00:52:32.730 --> 00:52:35.580
who think of Mao
as a philosopher?

00:52:35.580 --> 00:52:37.920
Is he venerated in his
country, or are they

00:52:37.920 --> 00:52:39.240
trying to discredit him?

00:52:44.175 --> 00:52:51.480
MARK BLITZ: We'll leave
Mother Teresa out of it.

00:52:51.480 --> 00:52:57.080
Yeah, I would say
a lot of this has

00:52:57.080 --> 00:52:59.990
to do with the
relative power of China

00:52:59.990 --> 00:53:04.590
and the resurgence of
that kind of activity

00:53:04.590 --> 00:53:07.220
and that kind of politics.

00:53:07.220 --> 00:53:10.280
There are fewer direct
Maoists than there were

00:53:10.280 --> 00:53:13.820
in the late '60s, early '70s.

00:53:13.820 --> 00:53:16.880
But there's still, I
think, a serious strain

00:53:16.880 --> 00:53:24.110
among certain people
of a kind of Maoism.

00:53:24.110 --> 00:53:26.750
But how far something
like that will go

00:53:26.750 --> 00:53:29.730
depends very much
on what happens,

00:53:29.730 --> 00:53:33.050
I think, in China itself.

00:53:33.050 --> 00:53:37.370
I don't come across,
even the colleagues

00:53:37.370 --> 00:53:42.740
of whom I'm not that fond,
dancing up and down to tell me

00:53:42.740 --> 00:53:44.960
how wonderful Mao is.

00:53:44.960 --> 00:53:47.270
But of course, I
would not be the one

00:53:47.270 --> 00:53:49.175
in front of whom they'd
perform such a dance.

00:53:49.175 --> 00:53:51.372
[LAUGHTER]

00:53:54.240 --> 00:53:55.560
AUDIENCE: Good evening, sir.

00:53:55.560 --> 00:53:57.810
Thank you so much for coming
to Hillsdale this evening

00:53:57.810 --> 00:53:59.520
for your discussion.

00:53:59.520 --> 00:54:02.140
In Steven Mosher's
book, The Bully of Asia,

00:54:02.140 --> 00:54:06.240
he draws attention to a comment
from Confucius that reads,

00:54:06.240 --> 00:54:09.240
"just as there are not
two suns in the sky,

00:54:09.240 --> 00:54:12.380
so there cannot be two
emperors on Earth."

00:54:12.380 --> 00:54:15.630
And he ties it into
ancient Chinese history.

00:54:15.630 --> 00:54:19.500
My question for you is,
as you understand him,

00:54:19.500 --> 00:54:23.010
does Confucian teaching endorse
the ambitions of conquest

00:54:23.010 --> 00:54:26.230
by brute force, as done
from the great unification

00:54:26.230 --> 00:54:27.730
to the disputes
with the West today?

00:54:33.610 --> 00:54:35.700
MARK BLITZ: Steven
Mosher will speak to you.

00:54:35.700 --> 00:54:38.550
So you can see what he says.

00:54:38.550 --> 00:54:39.270
I would say no.

00:54:39.270 --> 00:54:43.130
He doesn't endorse
conquest by brute force

00:54:43.130 --> 00:54:47.750
because his goals are not
really served by brute force.

00:54:47.750 --> 00:54:51.620
But his picture of
the best government

00:54:51.620 --> 00:54:57.560
was what you can consider a
whole empire over the territory

00:54:57.560 --> 00:54:58.310
that he knew best.

00:54:58.310 --> 00:55:03.410
It didn't really function
that way when he was alive.

00:55:03.410 --> 00:55:05.990
And who knows whether it ever
really functioned that way.

00:55:05.990 --> 00:55:09.260
But that was the government
that he liked best.

00:55:09.260 --> 00:55:15.140
So that there might be a
single empire over the known

00:55:15.140 --> 00:55:18.980
territory, could well be.

00:55:18.980 --> 00:55:22.130
If that needed to be
brought about by force,

00:55:22.130 --> 00:55:25.910
one way or the other, perhaps,
but not something, I think,

00:55:25.910 --> 00:55:29.090
that he would want to be
kept in place by force, kept

00:55:29.090 --> 00:55:33.180
in place by fear, but rather
by these other qualities

00:55:33.180 --> 00:55:34.462
he's discussing.

00:55:34.462 --> 00:55:36.772
AUDIENCE: Thank you, sir.

00:55:36.772 --> 00:55:38.962
MARK BLITZ: Sure.

00:55:38.962 --> 00:55:40.420
AUDIENCE: Confucius
would have been

00:55:40.420 --> 00:55:42.970
approximately a contemporary
of the prophet Daniel

00:55:42.970 --> 00:55:44.110
from the Old Testament.

00:55:44.110 --> 00:55:47.033
And Daniel would have had known
the history of the Hebrews,

00:55:47.033 --> 00:55:48.700
of course, and he
studied the Chaldeans.

00:55:48.700 --> 00:55:51.970
I just wondered whether you
are aware of any connection

00:55:51.970 --> 00:55:54.340
educationally, whether
Confucius could have

00:55:54.340 --> 00:55:57.970
gotten his ideas about virtue--

00:55:57.970 --> 00:56:00.400
and the principle
underpinnings of him

00:56:00.400 --> 00:56:03.370
seem to have a lot of parallels
with biblical history.

00:56:03.370 --> 00:56:05.336
And I just wonder if
you could address that.

00:56:05.336 --> 00:56:08.312
Thank you.

00:56:08.312 --> 00:56:11.130
MARK BLITZ: Well, I'm not
aware of any direct connection.

00:56:11.130 --> 00:56:12.910
And it would be
difficult because

00:56:12.910 --> 00:56:17.740
of the kind of isolation of
these places from each other.

00:56:17.740 --> 00:56:22.300
There are various arguments
to the effect that and which

00:56:22.300 --> 00:56:24.370
find it very
interesting that you

00:56:24.370 --> 00:56:29.350
have so many movements in
religion and even philosophy

00:56:29.350 --> 00:56:32.890
more or less around
the same time, making

00:56:32.890 --> 00:56:35.800
somewhat similar statements.

00:56:35.800 --> 00:56:40.760
But I doubt that there would
be any direct connection.

00:56:40.760 --> 00:56:44.330
But one can never say never.

00:56:44.330 --> 00:56:46.150
But none that I'm aware of.

00:56:46.150 --> 00:56:50.800
And I would doubt it, given the
range of things that Confucius

00:56:50.800 --> 00:56:55.270
knew of or didn't know
of, given he traveled,

00:56:55.270 --> 00:56:58.120
but the travel is
obviously limited.

00:56:58.120 --> 00:57:02.670
So you would have to find
something that somehow came

00:57:02.670 --> 00:57:05.860
and that he saw.

00:57:05.860 --> 00:57:08.950
And I think that's
not all that likely.

00:57:08.950 --> 00:57:11.410
A lot of his thought,
of course, resonates

00:57:11.410 --> 00:57:12.890
with things in the past.

00:57:12.890 --> 00:57:14.540
But some of it is novel as well.

00:57:14.540 --> 00:57:15.880
So that would be my view.

00:57:15.880 --> 00:57:21.175
But those things are always
subject to new discovery.

00:57:21.175 --> 00:57:22.630
NATALIE KENT: Thank
you Dr. Blitz.

00:57:22.630 --> 00:57:23.590
MARK BLITZ: Thank you.

00:57:23.590 --> 00:57:26.340
[APPLAUSE]

